       "$u(t)$ has non-zero average ($1/2$). Split into even and odd: $u(t) = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sgn}(t) \\leftrightarrow \\pi\\delta(\\omega) + \\frac{1}{j\\omega}$. $\\lim_{a\\to 0}e^{-at}u(t)$ is valid ONLY under distributional testing."
